WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.630
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Professor Paul Slovic, who teaches psychology

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:10.679
at the University of Oregon and also is president
of the Decision Sciences Research Institute

00:00:10.679 --> 00:00:12.349
in Eugene, Oregon.

00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:13.349
Really great having you on.

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:14.349
I appreciate your time.

00:00:14.349 --> 00:00:15.709
Speaker 2: Thank you, David.

00:00:15.709 --> 00:00:16.970
Good to be with you.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:24.220
Speaker 1: So maybe to start with, we make
decisions as humans about risk reward all

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.140
the time in our everyday lives.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.520
I think with many of the types of decisions
we make, we have the feeling that we're pretty

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:36.290
good at assessing the level of risk when we
are undertaking these decisions.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:42.460
Generally speaking, are humans as good as
we think at assessing risk or not?

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:51.360
Speaker 2: It depends if we're in a situation
that's familiar to us that we've, you know,

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.050
experienced before and and learn from our
mistakes and our successes, then I think we're

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.050
pretty, pretty good.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:07.720
If it's a novel situation where the stakes
are high, then we are a challenge, particularly

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:13.290
if the likelihood of something very good or
very bad happening is is small.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.750
And we have to kind of figure out, well, you
know, how do we how do we deal with this is

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:23.210
a risk that we're willing to take and that
it's beneficial for us to take.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.560
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, maybe to think through
an example, lots of people are afraid of flying

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.869
on commercial aircraft and there's all sorts
of different statistics that are often presented

00:01:32.869 --> 00:01:37.240
by those who extoll the safety record of commercial
aircraft.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.869
And sometimes they say, here is your risk
of death per million passenger miles flown

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:48.270
or the chance of a fatal crash is, you know,
some small number.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:52.850
And, you know, if you told me there was a
1% chance of a fatal crash, I'm not getting

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:53.850
on the airplane.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.229
I know that that's quite high.

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:56.909
I've taken hundreds of flights.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:57.909
Right.

00:01:57.909 --> 00:01:59.620
So one out of 100 sounds very high.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.640
But when the numbers are smaller, it does
become more difficult to think of what that

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.890
really means for us, doesn't it?

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.119
Speaker 2: Absolutely.

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:14.230
And more generally, the numbers really don't
communicate very well with us.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:21.110
What's more important is our is our experience,
you know, and have we flown and everything's

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.110
gone.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.110
Okay.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.050
And and what do we hear on our on television
or media?

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:32.330
Has it been a recent crash or has it been
has there been something that's happened that

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.900
leads us to have less trust in the safety
of of airliners?

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:43.290
Like, if there's a like the 737 a few years
ago were mysteriously crashing and and people

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.040
didn't know exactly what was going on or that
was scary.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:54.379
So so all of these things are a factor that
affects our what we call our feeling of risk.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:03:01.170
Because most of the time, we we take risks
depending on how we how we feel about it,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:09.150
whether whether we're worried or scared, as
opposed to whether the statistics, you know,

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.150
seem okay to us.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:11.150
Yeah.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.260
Speaker 1: I mean, thinking of the airplane
example, you often hear you're more likely

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.950
to get into an accident and die driving to
the airport than on the plane.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:24.239
But it certainly doesn't feel that way, I
think, to lots of people.

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:27.560
So that seems to be the psychological layer
to it.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.560
Speaker 2: Right.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.140
So there is a lot of things going on there.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:36.290
When you're when you're driving, you feel,
you know, you're in control of the risk and

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:40.159
you are partially in control, although not
as much as you think you are, because there

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:44.470
are other drivers on the roads that there
may be some bad conditions on the roadway

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:45.470
and so forth.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:48.090
But you feel in control when you're on the
plane.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.090
You know that.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:50.370
You know you're not controlling that plane.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:55.319
And so you have to trust the pilots and the
system.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:04:03.100
These days, many of the planes are being flown
by computer, but it doesn't you know, we don't

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.100
see that.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.890
Speaker 1: When it comes to something like
COVID 19.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:11.780
There's been a lot of interesting websites
where you can plug in a variety of different

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:17.470
data about yourself and about a scenario in
which you're thinking of putting yourself.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:23.759
And the idea is it will spit something out
to you objective that you can then use to

00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:27.169
determine whether it's a safe enough situation
for you or not.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:32.050
So, of course, we know age and preexisting
conditions are risk factors for getting very

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:33.669
sick with COVID.

00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:37.889
We also know that it spreads more easily indoors
than outdoors.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:43.069
And I did one of these before our interview,
and I can keep feeding information.

00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:44.069
Right.

00:04:44.069 --> 00:04:45.630
How many people am I going to be around?

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:46.849
Are they vaccinated?

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:48.610
Am I indoors or outdoors?

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.939
Will the encounter be one, two or three hour,
all these different things?

00:04:51.939 --> 00:04:57.680
And then it tells me your risk as a three
out of ten, it seems like it's very difficult

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.610
to really turn that into actionable intelligence
in a sense.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:07.249
I mean, it's it's so detailed, but at the
same time, what do I do with that information?

00:05:07.249 --> 00:05:08.590
Speaker 2: Absolutely.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.550
But first, I'm not sure how many people use
it.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:12.550
These sites.

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:20.089
Yeah, most of the time we're just operating
again on our experience and and how much how

00:05:20.089 --> 00:05:25.309
important it is for us to do, you know, to
have this meeting or to to go to this event,

00:05:25.309 --> 00:05:26.659
this sort of thing.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:31.630
And and again, we're using we're using our
feelings more than any statistics.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:32.630
Sure.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:36.839
We know that in general, if we're older or
we have a preexisting condition that makes

00:05:36.839 --> 00:05:38.930
us vulnerable, that we have to be more careful.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:45.759
And I think people are sensitive to that and
are trying to monitor that and and be careful.

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:51.080
But I think more generally, we as we see,
you know, very few people are taking precautions

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.080
right now.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:53.300
We see a few people wearing masks.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.530
And and it's like it's like it doesn't exist
anymore.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:59.899
Even though COVID is still still quite prevalent.

00:05:59.899 --> 00:06:05.379
You know, we're told that the the effects
are not as serious as the early versions.

00:06:05.379 --> 00:06:10.740
And and we feel pretty comfortable going about
our business as though things were normal,

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:11.740
even though they're not.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.909
But again, it's not a really a matter of statistics.

00:06:13.909 --> 00:06:16.809
It's a matter of how we feel about it.

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:22.419
And the thing about our feelings is sometimes
they they deceive us.

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:24.860
You know, we can't always trust them.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.199
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:31.499
The interesting thing about the the back to
normal COVID feelings that you talk about,

00:06:31.499 --> 00:06:36.849
you know, I don't remember if it's Daniel
Dennett or Daniel Kahneman who had a particular

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:39.749
heuristic that they wrote about.

00:06:39.749 --> 00:06:46.469
But there's this kind of familiarity where
once COVID is no longer something new and

00:06:46.469 --> 00:06:52.969
we've been living with the risk for a period
of time, even if we have not necessarily been

00:06:52.969 --> 00:06:57.860
actually engaging in risky behavior ourselves,
it sort of becomes background in a sense where

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.509
this is now part of the status quo of life.

00:07:00.509 --> 00:07:06.770
And it seems that may allow people to feel
as though the risk has lessened, even though

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:07.789
it really hasn't.

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:09.639
Maybe we've just gotten more used to it.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:13.190
Speaker 2: Yes, that that definitely happens.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:19.959
So I'm we're pretty comfortable with the seasonal
flu, you know, and even though it could be

00:07:19.959 --> 00:07:23.610
serious and a lot of people got very sick
and some died, you know, it was something

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:28.110
that we we lived with and became kind of normalized.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:32.869
So these things become normalized unless something
special happens.

00:07:32.869 --> 00:07:34.080
Just like gun violence.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:39.639
I mean, gun violence in this country has become
normalized.

00:07:39.639 --> 00:07:44.219
But every once in a while, we have some sort
of unusual and horrific event like we have

00:07:44.219 --> 00:07:49.339
in recent weeks in Highland Park, Illinois,
and Buffalo.

00:07:49.339 --> 00:07:54.009
That shakes us out of that and says, wait
a minute, you know, this is different.

00:07:54.009 --> 00:07:57.409
And we start to pay attention for a while.

00:07:57.409 --> 00:08:01.870
And then and then it's sort of our concern
fades away.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:07.360
There's sort of a window of when we're suddenly
awake to the problem and concerned about it

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.839
and want something done.

00:08:08.839 --> 00:08:13.039
And then and then over time, that window starts
to close.

00:08:13.039 --> 00:08:14.039
So.

00:08:14.039 --> 00:08:20.409
So, again, this is the way our feelings go,
you know, that that these events can shake

00:08:20.409 --> 00:08:24.629
us out of our, what we call our psychic numbing,
at least for a while.

00:08:24.629 --> 00:08:27.860
And that's the window when action needs to
be taken, it seems.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:33.610
Speaker 1: Also, there's the other side where,
for example, after 911, people's perception

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:41.810
of the risk of, for example, being on a hijacked
plane or being the victim of a terrorist attack

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:45.250
was also much higher than the real risk as
well.

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.480
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:53.860
And you know, what we know is that that if
the consequences are very, you know, imaginable

00:08:53.860 --> 00:09:00.480
and scary, you know, again, that that creates
a just thinking about those consequences,

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:06.440
sends a strong feeling of worry or anxiety,
you know, through into our brain.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:13.480
And then then it doesn't really matter what
the probability is that is that is our brain

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:19.810
can't can't moderate a feeling by a probability
unless you unless that probability is so low

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:21.360
that you could call it zero.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:22.360
Right.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.360
You don't worry about it.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.879
Well, as long as you got that feeling that's
driven by the consequences, you it feels as

00:09:28.879 --> 00:09:35.600
though it's likely and it doesn't matter whether
it's a 1% chance or a ten or a 40%, those

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.700
numbers don't matter because you're thinking
of the consequences and you're feeling at

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:40.700
risk.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.029
And we call it probability neglect.

00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:49.600
You know, that that we're not sensitive to
to the the different levels of it probability,

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.500
even though those are important, you know,
it really makes a difference if the chance

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:58.980
is one in a million or or one in ten, you
know, this sort of thing.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:04.939
But but it's hard for us where we're going
on our feelings to make those distinctions.

00:10:04.939 --> 00:10:10.630
We need to think analytically and and step
away from our feelings and think about, well,

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:15.899
what is this probability, you know, and and
and how likely is it and what's it compare

00:10:15.899 --> 00:10:20.430
to other probabilities that we that we encounter
and know something about.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:25.910
So it's a very complicated dance that our
brain does between feelings and reasoning.

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.290
Speaker 1: A couple other things I want to
ask you about.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.410
You mentioned this psychic numbing.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:38.319
One of the things that you have worked on
and elaborated is this idea of the psychophysical

00:10:38.319 --> 00:10:43.720
numbing, the idea that if people hear about
a level of loss of life, for example, that's

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:49.601
an extraordinarily large number, that it actually
becomes more difficult to connect to it, connect

00:10:49.601 --> 00:10:51.680
to it on an emotional level.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:56.350
And one of the things I've talked about from
a media analysis standpoint is that one of

00:10:56.350 --> 00:11:02.970
the reasons why a shooting with three victims
gets more media attention than death from

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:08.840
hunger in Africa is that the story has a defined
beginning, middle and end, and that makes

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:10.899
it good for corporate media.

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:16.019
But there's a whole other aspect to this that
relates to your work, which is to some degree,

00:11:16.019 --> 00:11:22.970
if you hear 7000 children a day are dying
from hunger, that it's we intuit that it's

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:26.009
an extremely high number, but we're not.

00:11:26.009 --> 00:11:32.290
Necessarily connecting to it empirically in
the way that we do to the three shooting deaths

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:33.290
to some degree.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:36.589
Is that roughly what you have found?

00:11:36.589 --> 00:11:44.180
Speaker 2: Yes, we we we have a saying that
that summarizes both what we've found in laboratory

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:48.110
research and what we know from looking outside
our window at what's going on.

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.930
And that is that the more who die, the less
we care.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:55.860
It's a rather startling realization.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.990
And that is, you know, that that that we we
care a lot about individual lives and we'll

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:07.249
do a lot to rescue someone, to protect an
individual, particularly if we're if we if

00:12:07.249 --> 00:12:12.269
we know their name, know them personally,
know their names, see their face and so forth.

00:12:12.269 --> 00:12:14.490
You get an emotional connection to that, that
life.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.680
And it's very important to protect.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.830
You don't get that emotional connection to
the statistics.

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.399
Speaker 1: Right.

00:12:21.399 --> 00:12:26.649
Speaker 2: Someone once said Statistics are
human beings with the tears dried off.

00:12:26.649 --> 00:12:27.649
No.

00:12:27.649 --> 00:12:33.610
And and so you have to you have to to work
at it to think about the individual lives

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:35.240
beneath the surface of those statistics.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:36.240
Yeah.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:40.220
Speaker 1: One of the things that some of
these charities that work on hunger in Africa

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:46.139
have done is they tell you that when you make
a contribution, you'll get pictures and information

00:12:46.139 --> 00:12:49.999
about the specific person that's being helped
by your donation.

00:12:49.999 --> 00:12:55.209
Whether it's true or not, it seems that they're
working on the idea that you're talking about

00:12:55.209 --> 00:12:56.209
here.

00:12:56.209 --> 00:13:01.750
Speaker 2: Yes, they recognized that, as do
reporters who often, you know, try to, you

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:08.240
know, humanize the story by talking about
individual victims in a larger tragedy like

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:13.500
the school shootings that you see up close
and personal, something about the individual

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:14.500
lives.

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:17.949
And and that that does that does make a difference.

00:13:17.949 --> 00:13:20.670
Speaker 1: Last thing I'd like to ask you
about.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:26.850
You've done work on the I believe it's called
the psychometric paradigm of risk perception,

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:33.149
which relates to if we have positive or negative
feelings about a certain activity, it affects

00:13:33.149 --> 00:13:35.910
our perception of how risky it is.

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:42.470
So if, for example, you really like motorcycle
B riding a motorcycle, you might perceive

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:47.680
that it is more safe actually to ride a motorcycle
than it is in reality.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.999
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:13:49.999 --> 00:13:57.040
Speaker 2: Yes, that again, results from our
relying on our feelings as opposed to statistics.

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:05.139
So if if, if, if you like something, you have
a positive feeling towards it, then then it

00:14:05.139 --> 00:14:06.139
feels safer.

00:14:06.139 --> 00:14:10.209
You know, it feels like the is very beneficial
and the risk is low.

00:14:10.209 --> 00:14:16.220
If you dislike something, then you're likely
to view it as high and risk and low in benefit

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.950
regardless of what the statistics say.

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:25.879
So for example, with regard to medicines I'm
sorry, with regard to chemicals, there are

00:14:25.879 --> 00:14:26.879
chemicals.

00:14:26.879 --> 00:14:31.829
We we tend to think are good chemicals like
medicines and bad chemicals like pesticides.

00:14:31.829 --> 00:14:32.829
Right.

00:14:32.829 --> 00:14:34.470
So we rate pesticides.

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:36.390
We start with our feeling that we dislike
them.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:39.120
And so we judge them as high and risk and
low and benefit.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.670
Even though pesticides have a lot of benefits.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.800
Same thing with medicines.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:47.430
We we tend to have more favorable view of
medicines.

00:14:47.430 --> 00:14:51.140
So we judge them as very beneficial and low
in risk, even though some medicines can be

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:52.180
very dangerous.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:53.180
So.

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:56.810
So it starts with our feelings and then we
derive our sense of risk and benefit from

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:57.810
those feelings.

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:01.870
Speaker 1: What's the right way to assess
risk if there is such a sense?

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:03.579
I mean, do we need experts?

00:15:03.579 --> 00:15:09.959
Are expert assessments better than a layperson's
or how can we sort of account for all of these

00:15:09.959 --> 00:15:10.959
biases?

00:15:10.959 --> 00:15:19.560
Speaker 2: The right way is to is to try to
learn when we can trust our feelings and when

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:20.870
we when we can't.

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:22.939
And we just discussed situations.

00:15:22.939 --> 00:15:26.730
We can't trust our feelings because when the
number is static, we get statistics that are

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:28.779
large and complex.

00:15:28.779 --> 00:15:33.490
The other is we need to we need to rely on
experts, you know.

00:15:33.490 --> 00:15:34.490
Absolutely.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:41.149
We need to to kind of but we also need to
to evaluate what we're hearing with our feelings.

00:15:41.149 --> 00:15:44.740
Don't know does does it what the person is
saying?

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:45.820
Does it feel right?

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:46.970
Is there are there sources?

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:51.079
Are there data, you know, does that seem valid
and so forth?

00:15:51.079 --> 00:15:56.160
We need both of these ways of thinking what
Kahneman calls fast thinking, you know, with

00:15:56.160 --> 00:16:01.970
our gut feelings and slow thinking with, you
know, analysis and trust in experts.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:02.970
We need to do that.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:08.930
We need to we need to look for trusted experts
to help us in these complex situations.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:14.740
Speaker 1: We've been speaking with Dr. Paul
Slovic, who's a professor of psychology at

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:17.060
the University of Oregon.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.160
Really appreciate your insights and your time
today.

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:20.599
Speaker 2: Thank you very much, David.

